 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟳/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗔𝗱𝘃𝗮𝗻𝗰𝗲𝗱 𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽𝘀 & 𝗖𝗼𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲𝘅 𝗗𝗮𝘁𝗮 𝗦𝘁𝗿𝘂𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲𝘀

Continuing my #30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I went deeper into Ansible loops and learned how to work with lists, dictionaries, and structured data.

🔄 𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗧𝗵𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗴𝗵 𝗟𝗶𝘀𝘁𝘀

Instead of repeating tasks, we can process multiple values with a single task:

- name: Create users
  ansible.builtin.user:
    name: "{{ item }}"
    state: present
  loop:
    - devuser
    - testuser
    - appuser

📦 𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗧𝗵𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗴𝗵 𝗗𝗶𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗲𝘀

Real-world automation often involves structured data:

users:
  - name: devuser
    shell: /bin/bash
  - name: appuser
    shell: /bin/sh

We can access individual values using:

- name: Create users
  ansible.builtin.user:
    name: "{{ item.name }}"
    shell: "{{ item.shell }}"
    state: present
  loop: "{{ users }}"

🎯 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗖𝗼𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲𝘅 𝗗𝗮𝘁𝗮 𝗦𝘁𝗿𝘂𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲𝘀?

In production environments, configuration isn't always a simple list.

We may need to manage:

☁️ Multiple environments
👥 Multiple users
🌐 Multiple servers
📦 Multiple applications
🔐 Different configurations

Using structured variables keeps our playbooks clean, reusable, and scalable.

🔍 𝗨𝘀𝗲𝗳𝘂𝗹 𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗿𝗼𝗹𝘀

Ansible also provides loop controls such as:

loop_control:
  label: "{{ item.name }}"

This makes task output easier to understand when working with complex objects.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Simple loops are great for repetition.

Structured loops are powerful for real-world automation.

The better we understand Ansible's data structures, the easier it becomes to build flexible and maintainable automation. 🚀
